24. the decision: a collision earns no candidate field
  PASS  a collision's constraint is NEGATIVE, and its satisfying set is the dictionary -- orders of magnitude past anything offerable
          does: rhyme field 1265, NOT-rhyme 38158 (96.79% of 39423); ear: rhyme field 1661, NOT-rhyme 37762 (95.79% of 39423); will: rhyme field 1758, NOT-rhyme 37665 (95.54% of 39423); floor: rhyme field 1235, NOT-rhyme 38188 (96.87% of 39423). The smallest complement is 37665 words = 1569x `offered`=24, the size of a field a writer is actually handed. `joint_field` intersects POSITIVE calls and gets a set a writer can read; the complement of one is a copy of the lexicon, so there is no field to hand over
  PASS  and doctrine 9's mechanism on top of it forbids the six commonest words in English
          the modal head of ~98% of the lexicon is ['you', 'i', 'the', 'to', 'a', "'s"] (WIRED 2026-08-11: lex.freq_rank now reads data/opensubtitles_en_50k.tsv, not the old web crawl). The modal exclusion exists to push a writer off the predictable RHYME; over a negative constraint it has no rhyme class to be modal IN, at ANY frequency distribution -- and a collision has no call word, so the call-conditional table `joint_field` now prefers has nothing to be consulted on either. The defect is the POLARITY of the constraint, not the ranking over it
  PASS  so a line whose only findings are collisions is briefed with no words at all — and doctrine 7 is the second reason
          8 such line(s). The loop is a FLOOR: rejection, not selection. A collision on a draft with zero violations is not a rejection, so offering replacements would be ordering the permitted region -- and would make the harness decide that an unmandated rhyme is a defect, which is often the best thing in a song

